<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" align="center" class="tborder" style="border-bottom: 0;">
	<tr class="titlebg">
		<td valign="middle" align="left" width="15%" style="padding-left: 6px;">
			<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/topic/normal_post.gif" alt="" />
			Author
		</td>
		<td valign="middle" align="left" width="85%" style="padding-left: 6px;">
			Topic: Demon Hunter's &nbsp;(Read 170 times)
		</td>
	</tr>
</table>
<table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" class="bordercolor">
	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=63" title="View the profile of Mordread">Mordread</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Orc Pawn<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Gender: <img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/Male.gif" alt="Male" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 37<br />
								<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/YaBBImages/avatars/spawn.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								Guardian of the Crimson Flame<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=63"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="mailto:mordread_@hotmail.com"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/email_sm.gif" alt="Email" title="Email" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=121.msg377#msg377"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=121.msg377#msg377">Demon Hunter's</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b> on:</b> April 10, 2003, 02:27:22 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							This story was not written by me, it was written by the late Froglok Ranger, Ousted King of the Froglok, Sithrin. A bard at heart and a dear lost friend. Some names you may know, some may be unknown. Some names have a new spirit (Veranna sold her account and is now played by someone else). But I wanted to share this story with you all, and honor the memory of the Lost King!<br /><br />***<br /><br />Demon Hunters<br /><br />Callador stared down into the bubbling lava, eyes narrowed against the bloody<br />glare. &quot;Looks hot,&quot; he muttered. Already sweat was beginning to form beneath<br />his armor, the brisk fall breezes stifled by the sulfurous heat of the lava. He<br />squinted at Sithrin through the heat haze. &quot;You're sure there's a demon in<br />here?&quot;<br /><br />The ranger shrugged and adjusted the straps of his boots. &quot;That's what Ged<br />says.&quot;<br /><br />Nodding, Callador pulled off his helm and shook his hair out, cooling himself<br />only slightly in the process. &quot;Then I'm in, it is the will of Rodcet Nife.&quot;<br /><br />Back from the edge of the caldera, Mordread spread his hands and shrugged. <br />&quot;When have I failed to accompany my friends into the jaws of death?&quot; The mage's<br />eyes sparkled as he began preparing his array of spells, slowly working the<br />runes of magic over in his mind. A gust of heated air tugged at the flowing<br />black robes he wore, and the metallic threads around its hem glinted in the<br />ruddy light.<br /><br />Sithrin grunted then, his senses suddenly aware of a change in the environment<br />as magical portals opened not far from where he crouched. &quot;The druids are<br />here.&quot; A small clatter of shale heralded the approach of the two priestesses,<br />the jagged pebbles bouncing and sliding down into the lava which consumed them<br />silently. Sithrin shivered despite the heat, forcing a smile as he greeted the<br />new arrivals.<br /><br />Teynara, tall and leather-clad, slung her hammer over one shoulder and leaned<br />against the tunnel entrance, a slight smile playing across her lips. Veranna<br />stalked in just behind her, her lithe wood-elven walk lending her an air of<br />feline grace, the scimitar at her hip seeming too large. Sithrin smiled at the<br />thought, she was nearly as deadly with a blade as he with his own weapons.<br /><br />&quot;Well, then, now that we're all here . . &quot;<br /><br />A fist-sized rock bounced off Callador's helm, and the priest jerked back from<br />the edge of the lava, cursing, and more rocks suddenly cascaded past the tunnel<br />entrance, plumes of lava rising in the volcano to meet them.<br /><br />&quot;Heeeeeelp meeeee . . .&quot; A wood elf suddenly bounced into view, tumbling<br />perilously near the edge of the volcano's mouth as the wall crumbled beneath<br />him. Four voices rose in disparate chanting as the words of spells began<br />forming, and the flat twang of a bowstring echoed in the narrow tunnel.<br /><br />An arrow drilled through the wood elf's jerkin, pinning it to the soft shale<br />near the edge of the volcano. Spells swirled around him at the same instant,<br />slowly lifting him back to safety. He clung to the rock as the magic released<br />its hold on him, his breath coming in ragged gasps.<br /><br />&quot;Thank you . .. . friends . .&quot; he panted, slowly regaining his feet. &quot;Too . .<br />. close.&quot;<br /><br />&quot;What brings you out to this desolate land?&quot; Mordread's voice, low and calm,<br />seemed to bring the wood elf some measure of peace.<br /><br />&quot;I have heard tales . .. but I get ahead of myself. I am Opus, wandering bard<br />and itinerant adventurer.&quot; Grinning, the bard bowed with a flourish and tapped<br />a staccato rhythm on the drums hanging from his hip. &quot;As I was saying, I had<br />heard of many vile creatures lurking within this area, and thought I should see<br />them for myself. The better to recreate them in songs and stories.&quot;<br /><br />Sithrin grunted and quickly exchanged glances with the others, then turned back<br />to Opus. &quot;We're heading into Solusek's Eye, ourselves. You're welcome to come<br />along if you don't mind the heat.&quot;<br /><br />With a soft chuckle, Opus clasped the ranger's hand, and the six prepared to<br />enter the lair of the goblins.<br /><br />* * *<br /><br />&quot;I really do not like the looks of this.&quot; Standing at the edge of a narrow<br />precipice, the ranger stared down into a roiling mass of molten stone. A narrow<br />arch of glossy obsidian stretched over the deadly stew, gracefully coming to<br />rest on another ledge some distance across from the party.<br /><br />Veranna chuckled and patted Sithrin's arm. &quot;It's just lava, Sith, get going.&quot;<br /><br />Gingerly, Sithrin moved across the arch, sweat stinging his eyes. His breath<br />leaked out in a sigh of relief as his foot touched the granite ledge.<br /><br />&quot;Half-elf scum!&quot; The goblin burst through a yawning gap in the wall to<br />Sithrin's right, and the ranger ducked back as a spear sent sparks flying near<br />his right ear. Behind him, he could hear his friends preparing spells, and<br />Opus' voice rising in an oddly keening melody.<br /><br />&quot;I hate lava.&quot; Sithrin spun inside the goblin's spear-thrust, his whip cracking<br />as it tore a chunk from the goblin's face. Another goblin sprung from the guard<br />post to join the first, forcing the ranger back a step, cursing. A blast of<br />metal shards ripped through the air overhead, Mordread's strident chanting<br />directing it into the mass of goblins forming on the ledge ahead.<br /><br />Taking advantage of the distraction, Sithrin plunged into the churning<br />confusion, his dirk driving into the wounded while his whip bit into those too<br />slow to give way. The druids and priest leapt into the gap he'd created, their<br />hammers and blades hewing into goblins. With a grunt, Sithrin slammed his<br />forehead into the face of a charging flame goblin, and the creature fell back<br />screaming into the lava. Used to the heat or not, the goblins burned just the<br />same.<br /><br />Veranna ducked beneath a crudely forged short sword and drove the honed edge of<br />her scimitar up into her attacker's gullet, pushing the creature back into the<br />path of Teynara's deadly hammer. Bones shattered, and the goblin collapsed,<br />it's head a pulpy ruin. The last goblin turned to run, but Sithrin's whip<br />slashed through its hamstring, dropping it to its knees. With a savage howl,<br />Callador whipped his great hammer around, snapping through the creature's spine<br />with such force that its ribs ruptured and burst through its sides.<br /><br />The bloodied adventurers ducked into the guard station, quickly scanning their<br />surroundings for lurking goblins. Spying none, they quickly moved on, weapons<br />drawn, eyes probing the thick shadows cast from the guttering torches mounted on<br />the walls. The crudely carved cave walls amplified and distorted sounds, and<br />odd shadows flitted from corner to corner, so that the explorers did not see the<br />goblins guarding the bridge until it was nearly too late.<br /><br />One of the foul creatures shouted, and turned to run for reinforcements, but<br />Sithrin's command of nature stood him in good stead as he directed strands of<br />fungus to wrap tight around the ankles of the runner, slowing its progress to a<br />crawl. A shaman stirred its mystical powers to direct spirits of plague toward<br />the ranger, but a sharp command from Opus glazed the creature's eyes. With a<br />deft series of magical chords, the bard directed the wicked caster against its<br />one-time allies. As one the druids fell upon the back of the snared goblin, and<br />Sithrin crossed blades with a cinder goblin as it leapt onto the bridge. <br />Veranna whispered words of natural power, and a swarm of insects fell upon the<br />goblins, devouring their flesh as they writhed in torment.<br /><br />With a wink, Veranna led the group past the half-consumed corpses, Mordread<br />taking up the rear and wrinkling his nose at the bloody mess atop the bridge. <br />&quot;Wouldn't hurt you people to be a little neater,&quot; he grumbled, shaking his head.<br /><br />As the party wound its way deeper into the basalt maze of the goblins' fortress,<br />they encountered only token resistance. Goblins sprang from hiding, only to be<br />hacked down again, their foul blood steaming on the hot stones of the caverns'<br />floor. &quot;Something's not right . . .&quot; Teynara began to speak, but Sithrin's<br />suddenly wide eyes and flaring nostrils cut her short.<br /><br />The ranger knelt, feeling the life of the stone beneath him, and he opened<br />himself to Karana's awareness of all things. His hackles rose then, and his<br />voice became clotted with hatred for the unnatural. &quot;The demon is near.&quot; <br />Rising from his knees, the half-elf seemed to grow in stature, his lips curled<br />back in a feral snarl. &quot;This way.&quot;<br /><br />His companions followed, but Opus cocked his head to one side, listening<br />intently to the strange echoes that bounded around the hunters. &quot;Sithrin, the<br />sounds here are off, perhaps -&quot;<br /><br />The ranger lowered his shoulder and smashed into the door ahead of him, howling<br />with Karana's thunder. Callador leapt in behind him, his will seething with<br />anger at the thought of an infernal demon so near to him. The pair crashed into<br />the goblins that guarded the doorway, and Mordread's arcane powers flashed and<br />roared between them. Whirling shards of metal hammered the goblins to their<br />knees, and the druids sprang into the room, weapons spinning into the dazed<br />goblins. Bones snapped and flesh parted before the onslaught, and the goblins<br />fell back, wailling in fear and anger.<br /><br />Callador communed with the power of the Prime Healer, drawing forth divine<br />strength to heal the wounds of those around him. Opus stalked the room quietly,<br />listening to the walls, tapping his fingers lightly against the stone. &quot;Sithrin<br />. . .&quot;<br /><br />The goblins burst through hidden doors, their howls raw with focused rage. <br />&quot;Death to the invaders!&quot;<br /><br />&quot;Ambush!&quot; Opus shouted and dove for cover, his hands finding his lute as he<br />began picking out the enchanted chords that would bind at least one of the enemy<br />to do his bidding. Mordread fell back to the wall, his hands drawing arcane<br />sigils in the air as the tide of goblins crashed into the room. Callador was<br />flung back by an lucky stroke, the side of his helm ringing with the impact as<br />he staggered away from the fray.<br /><br />In the chaotic swirl of battle, Sithrin found Teynara and Veranna to either side<br />of him, forming a loose triangle as they glared out at the encroaching goblins. <br />Their weapons flared in the uncertain lighting, cleaving through the crimson<br />flesh of the goblins and shattering bones until the floor was slick with gore,<br />and their arms burned with effort. But the goblins were falling back, beaten<br />away by force of arms, and the streaming death of Mordread's magic. Callador<br />quickly set to the work of healing the wounded, knitting closed injuries with<br />the power of his faith alone. The goblins snarled at the edges of the room, but<br />dared advance no further.<br /><br />Teynara studied the goblins, trying to peer past them into the red mists<br />clogging the tunnel they had come through. &quot;Why don't they flee or attack? <br />What are they waiting for?&quot;<br /><br />Veranna's fingers twitched through the patterns that would invoke her connection<br />to nature, her voice a raspy whisper. &quot;Their master.&quot; A shadow swelled within<br />the infernal mists, and Veranna unleashed her spell in its direction.<br /><br />Pandemonium reigned. Sithrin found himself cut off from his companions, helpless<br />to reach them through the wall of goblins as the demon pounced upon Opus,<br />smashing the bard against the stone and ripping at his flesh with stony talons. <br />Teynara and Veranna hacked at the demon's back, their blows rending its flesh,<br />but still it kept after Opus. The bard fumbled with his lute, but could not<br />manage the concentration required to perform the magic of his songs.<br /><br />A spear blasted through the bard's side, and he slumped against the wall. His<br />blood gushed in a crimson arc, and the demon laughed, it's long, forked tongue<br />lapping droplets from the air. &quot;Now you are mine.&quot;<br /><br />A wolf's howl tore through the room, and Sithrin vaulted over the goblins facing<br />him, his dagger taking one in the throat as his whip tore the eye from another. <br />He landed behind the demon and flailed his weapons mightily across the thing's<br />exposed back. Shrieking in rage it swept wide its wings, driving the goblins<br />around it to their knees.<br /><br />The druids took advantage of the distraction to pool their powers, Veranna<br />calling up swarms of insects which burrowed into the wounds of the dazed<br />goblins, wracking them with horrific pain. Teynara focused the soothing balm of<br />Tunare into Opus' wounds, even as Callador encompassed the fallen bard with a<br />glowing shield of faith.<br /><br />The ranger and the demon circled slowly, each seeking an opening in the defenses<br />of the other. Feints were thrown and batted aside as the two began the dance of<br />death. Whip and dirk tested the strength of scales and talons, but neither<br />could find their way through to strike a telling blow.<br /><br />&quot;I would toy with you, ranger, but your friends will gather their strength again<br />soon, and I do not like those odds.&quot; A savage scream tore free from the demon<br />then, and the battle cries of goblins rose in answer from all around. The<br />sounds of their approach thundered through the stone, and the demon flapped<br />upward.<br /><br />Snarling, Sithrin lashed his whip around the demon's leg, struggling to pull the<br />creature down to finish what they had started before the reinforcements could<br />arrive. But already the wounded goblins they had beaten back were rising to<br />their feet, swatting down the last of the insects Veranna had summoned.<br /><br />&quot;Veranna, get us out of here.&quot; Mordread's voice was strained as he stared down<br />the horde of goblins charging up one tunnel.<br /><br />&quot;I haven't the strength!&quot; She cried, and sank to her knees, flattening her<br />palms to the stone in an effort to draw forth more of Tunare's power.<br /><br />The mage shouted again, but his voice was lost in the cries of the goblins who<br />drove him off his feet with their hellish charge. Mordread lashed the goblins<br />with streams of magical force, snapping bones and rupturing organs with the<br />strength of his castings. Callador waded into the melee, his warhammer<br />flattening goblins as he worked towards his fallen friend. Teynara pulled Opus<br />to his feet, and the bard drew axe and sword to aid in the battle. Veranna's<br />scimitar became a blur as she whirled to her feet, taking down the nearest<br />goblin before its spear could pierce her side.<br /><br />Sithrin and the demon grappled, weapons forgotten as they struggled to find one<br />another's throats and crush the life there. The demon snapped its teeth closed<br />an hairsbreadth from Sithrin's eye, but the ranger jerked his head to the side<br />and bit back, rending the demon's ear from its head. With a ferocious burst of<br />strength, the ranger drove his knee into his foe's groin and chopped a hand<br />across its throat. The creature convulsed in pain, hurling Sithrin away. <br />Goblins fell on him, pinning his arms before he could regain his feet, bearing<br />him back from the combat.<br /><br />From beneath a knot of howling goblins, Mordread's arm burst into view, blasting<br />goblins blindly as he struggled to his feet. Veranna kicked and spit, smashing<br />the pommel of her scimitar into the temple of one goblin as she kicked goblin<br />corpses from atop Mordread, freeing the mage to unleash corruscating shards of<br />energy against the goblins. The raw power of his assault splattered the walls<br />and ceiling with gobbets of sizzling goblin flesh.<br /><br />Sithrin howled in rage and surged to his feet, goblins clinging to him. Opus<br />and Callador waded through the fight to his side, well-aimed blows knocking<br />goblins from his back and arms, freeing him as he recovered his weapons. But<br />the odds were growing worse, and the demon was recovering for another attack. <br />Teynara struggled to control the flux of a magical gateway as goblins swarmed<br />toward her, battering at her defenses.<br /><br />Sense pricked by the marshalling of such arcane natural forces, Veranna took<br />stock of the situation. Besieged as she was by goblins, Teynara's strength was<br />already flagging from distracting wounds and the effort of crafting such a<br />difficult spell. With a wildcat's shriek, Veranna hurled herself into the knot<br />of goblins, blade flashing as her combat spells flared to life. She could hear<br />Teynara's casting continue, but the sudden cold of a blade slashing between her<br />ribs focused her thoughts on the more immediate needs of survival. The druidess<br />hacked into the neck of a charging goblin, her warcry keening from between<br />blood-spattered lips as the goblin's spear impaled her thigh.<br /><br />With a hungry grin, the demon lunged towards the injured druid, tongue flicking<br />the air before it in anticipation. Opus and Sithrin lunged for the demon in the<br />same movement, their weapons striving towards the creature's breast as it rose<br />above the goblins. Pain lanced through the bard as blades bit into his arms and<br />legs, Sithrin vomited blood as a spear skewered his back. Mordread struggled<br />to his feet, and the ranger caught the despair in his bloody eyes. They had come<br />so far . . . .<br /><br />Sithrin turned his face up to the demon, and as his blood coursed out to mingle<br />with the torn flesh of the goblins, he spat his disdain. And then the world<br />went away . . .<br /><br /><br />* * *<br /><br />Consciousness came back to Opus in a series of stabbing pains. It seemed a<br />hundred wounds all vied for his attention, and for a brief moment, he wished he<br />had been left to die. He had been torn between tears of joy and bitter sorrow<br />when he had seen Teynara's hands work through the spell which had borne the<br />heroes out of danger. He would live, but it meant dealing with the pain of his<br />wounds. He groaned and struggled to sit, but firm hands held him down.<br /><br />&quot;You are in the hands of the druids of Tunare now, you are safe.&quot;<br /><br />&quot;And my friends?&quot;<br /><br />&quot;Though their blood has stained our circle, all will survive to fight another<br />day.&quot;<br /><br />Opus smiled, and sleep claimed him once more.<br /><br />***<br />And so ends the tale of the Demon Hunters, long live their memory!
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
								</td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/ip.gif" alt="" border="0" /> Logged
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />Mordread Draconius<br />Arch Convoker of Cascade Point
						</td>
					</tr>
				</table>
			</td></tr>
		</table>
	</td></tr>
</table>
